Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show  where we track the changes happening to  our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions  we're a week into the olympics and  there's a warning that these games could  be the hottest  ever held extreme heat is nothing new in  tokyo  but experts say the role of  human-induced climate change  is making it worse now according to the  japanese meteorological agency  japan and more specifically tokyo is  warming much  faster than the rest of the planet the  mean temperature in the country's  capital  has increased by 2.86 degrees  since 1900 that's nearly three times  faster than the world's average  and part of the reason tokyo experiences  such high temperatures  is because of the urban heat island  effect this is where an  urban area is significantly warmer than  its surrounding rural areas  due to heat being created by all the  people transport and buildings  it means that during the night the  surface of the largely urbanized tokyo  area  releases heat that's been trapped during  the day preventing the city from cooling  now heat waves are a natural part of the  climate system  however human-induced global warming is  making them stronger  and more frequent scientists predict  that extremely hot temperatures in japan  will become the new normal if the  world's temperature increases by one and  a half degrees celsius  above pre-industrial levels now in a  sporting context  a hot and humid environment can  represent a risk to the performance and  health of spectators  officials and of course athletes as  sky's tom parmenter reports from tokyo  it is sweltering and every outdoor sport  is feeling it  we've had athletes collapsing and some  events moved away from the middle of the  day  we got here it was a bit of a shock i'm  not gonna lie but yeah so you're getting  used to it  at home we were in greenhouses hot pods  just kind of sweat on with um layers as  well math face masks training face masks  so as soon as we got here and it  definitely felt like that  as much as the heat the humidity is  sapping their strength  it is punishing out there and each of  them have to carefully manage it so  there's more drinks more breaks  they could easily overcook themselves  this was johnny brownlee's preparation  chamber  in his conservatory in yorkshire yes  that is sweat  but when it's for real there is no  escape it's not an unusually hot summer  in tokyo but researchers here have  calculated that the average temperature  has increased faster here than in other  parts of the world  up three degrees over the past century  now with the athletics underway the  middle and long distance runners  have to find their climate control it's  just  so much harder uh straight in the body  on the heart rate everything is just  more increased and it's actually just  the perception of  not being able to cool down as well  which is quite difficult to  so not only is it physically i think  mentally sometimes you need to be  i suppose reassured that you're not just  going to implode  the hot rain that sometimes drops in  doesn't clear the humidity  we're on the 28th floor and you can't  see any of tokyo now and then it clears  again  the midsummer games here are tough but  the heat  is just one of the many challenges tom  parmenter  sky news tokyo well joining me now is  melissa wilson  british rower and member of champions  for earth melissa thank you very much  indeed  for joining us now it's just three years  ago that a heatwave in japan claimed the  lives of more than a thousand people  thousands were hospitalized again last  summer how much of a concern was the  weather and the heat for  athletes competing at tokyo  yeah a huge concern both for the  athletes and for  their coaching teams and physiology  teams behind them as we heard from  some of the long-distance runners just  then people have been in heat chambers i  know the  sailing squads the hockey teams have  been looking at that and alice deering  in the open water she's preparing for  swimming in water that over 10  kilometers that is going to be  at least 29 degrees so she's been in  these tanks of hot water trying to train  and acclimatize  just to face the competition that she's  going to have this summer  and how much does climate change concern  you when it comes to competing  your sport an outdoor event rowing uh  it's not just heat that impacts you but  also potentially extreme weather which  we're seeing  a lot more of yeah  absolutely we had two days of racing  postponed because of the typhoon that  came in  and i was talking to one of the tokyo  rose this morning he came away with the  silver medal and he was saying in terms  of heat  just for their half hour training  sessions which is really quite a light  load  easy steady state they were losing up to  2  liters of water just in that half hour  period in their training in the week  leading up to the game  so it's definitely something that will  weigh on the minds of these athletes and  especially when they're trying to make  strategic decisions as well in the  course of a rowing race  it's hard to keep that clarity of  thought when your body physiologically  is under such extreme pressures  well melissa we have really appreciated  having your thoughts today thanks very  much indeed for joining us  in today's other climate news enough ice  melted in greenland  to cover florida with two inches of  water in just  a day the world meteorological  organizations say the country lost more  than  eight and a half billion tons of surface  mass from an ice melt  on tuesday scientists predict  greenland's ice melt will raise sea  levels between  2 and 10 centimeters by the end of the  century putting coastal cities  at risk of flooding  environmentalists say pollution has  caused this lagoon in argentina to turn  pink look at that the pink hue is  thought to be caused by waste sodium  sulfite  used by fish farms to clean shrimp now  while the striking shade of pink may  look pretty  water treatment specialists say the  contamination is killing the chance of  any kind of life  surviving in the water and the natural  history museum has opened a new  exhibition on the consequences of  climate change  and the potential solutions director  doug gur told sky news the museum  will walk the walk on its promise to  reach net zero by 2035.  and to find out more you can listen to  the interview with mr gur on this week's  episode of climatecast and you can find  that wherever you normally get your  podcasts  countries in africa are being asked to  make big climate commitments ahead of  the cop 26 summit in november  now these include reducing emissions and  investment in renewable energy  in order to keep the level of global  warming to one and a half degrees above  pre-industrial levels now according to  the united nations africa is the  continent  most vulnerable to the impacts of  climate change  countries there face a wide range of  impacts including increased drought and  floods  as well as decreasing food production  and loss of biodiversity  but despite being so vulnerable to the  effects of climate change  africa is not a significant source of  greenhouse gas emissions  you can see here emissions from regions  worldwide from 1965  all the way through to 2020 and asia  pacific  is by far the biggest contributor in  recent years  with china alone responsible for nearly  60 percent of those  followed by north america and then  europe in comparison  africa has consistently contributed a  very small percentage of co2 emissions  the presidents of kenya and malawi have  spoken to sky news  and explained what they think the  pressing issues are ahead of cop 26.  we were all in paris a few years ago  the enormous pledges for  adaptation and mitigation that were made  and here we are now heading to glasgow  with very few of those commitments  actually lived upon  we need to be able to get to a situation  where we walk the talk  and do what we say and my hope and my  prayer is that  moving into glasgow we are going to get  more realism and that is why i'm saying  we need all parties at the table  this issue of climate change has  affected us  and our irregular rain patterns have  affected us badly but through it all  we are having to take measures where  food sufficiency becomes  foremost let's bring in our africa  correspondent john sparks  john thanks very much indeed for joining  us now of course one of the big things  is food security can you put into  context for us how climate change is  impacting people's lives in africa  already  that's right it's interesting isn't it  sally food security probably not the  first thing  you think of in the developed world when  you talk about climate change but it  sure is  here in africa the fact is tens of  millions of people  on the continent live life on the edge  they live a hand-to-mouth  existence and when weather patterns  change people go hungry  and we saw that in south sudan recently  rains have washed out hundreds of  kilometers of  of prime farmland and that water had  been accumulating since 2018.  nobody had any seen anything like it and  in madagascar currently the world food  program  is saying that entire communities are  teetering on the edge  of starvation after a severe two-year  drought really serious problem and as a  consequence the world's  poorest countries they want loans and  grants  to help fight and adapt to climate  change and they're becoming increasingly  vocal about it as well but the developed  world still haven't met  their commitments in terms of 100  billion  dollars of climate finance and that was  a target that was set back in 2009  so there sure is a lot to do that  certainly is john thanks very much  indeed for joining us  staying in africa now because tomorrow  is world rangers day  a day to recognize the work being done  to conserve the environment  now team lioness is one of kenya's first  all-female wildlife  ranger units the group used their  relationships with massify women in the  area  to protect their national park from harm  what is your name my name is piriti  lakara  trim learning  it was my vision to come and protect  regulation future  we wake up at 5 00 a.m  we go for morning run after we take  baths we take cheese then  we arrange ourselves for the patrol  here we see the footprint of algebra  [Music]  cleanliness and can lead to drought and  this drought can cause  some wildlife like lions and elephants  to change their position they can move  from  the national park to around the  communities  in search of water and pastures animals  like lion can enter into  bombers they kill their livestocks and  this leads to human wildlife conflict  [Music]  then if they go to interact with ladies  in the  in our community they can't get  information  easily this is a  horn of an envelope maybe it has  brought when they are fighting with each  other  for us as ladies we can graduate the  community and get  information easily because we live with  them like our mothers our sisters  so it is very easy for us to interact  with them and to get information  i hope that in the future many women  will be  empowered and i'm hopeful for the future  of the land that  i am protecting i am hoping that maybe  deforestation will not be there  because we protect all species of  wildlife and plants also  and finally a little happy news trail  cameras have captured footage  of the first baby beaver born in norfolk  for 600 years  now the beaver known as a kit was  spotted swimming at  wild ken hill a stretch of land being  restored to its natural state  beavers once native to norfolk have been  reintroduced to the area  because their dams create ponds and  streams in turn  restoring local wetlands well that's  everything from us for this week don't  forget you can keep up with all the  climate news over the weekend on our  website  just head to sky news dot com forward  slash climate  thanks very much indeed for joining us  i'll see you next week  [Music]

